TITLE:: Break
summary:: The breakbeat registry — what the \break engine knows about your drum loops
categories:: Live Coding>Synesthesia
related:: Guides/Breaks, Guides/Synesthesia, Reference/Synesthesia-Reference, Classes/NodeProxy, Classes/GrainBuf

DESCRIPTION::

Break is the language-side half of the breakbeat machine. It scans folders of
drum-loop recordings, works out each one's tempo and length in beats, loads them as
mono buffers, and holds the per-voice state the code::\break:: SynthDef needs —
the slice-sequence buffers and the grain-envelope windows. A large sample library
is indexed instead (code::addLibrary::): every loop is known at boot, and each is
loaded the first time something plays it.

The split exists because of the def contract in this system (see the repo's
code::Setup/SynthDefs/CONVENTIONS.md::, rule 5): a SynthDef may not reference
interpreter state. So nothing on the server can know a sample's tempo, index your
library, or hold a rewritable sequence. All of that lives here, and the engine is
pure DSP driven by controls.

The tutorial is link::Guides/Breaks::. You rarely call these methods directly —
the vocabulary on link::Classes/NodeProxy:: (code::brk::, code::grind::,
code::seq::…) and link::Classes/Symbol:: (code::chop::, code::bpm_::) is the
performance surface. What follows is the API underneath it.

note::
Buffers live on the server, so a server reboot invalidates the library. Re-run
code::Break.scan:: and code::Break.addLibrary:: (or your Setup file) after
rebooting.
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: scan
Scan a samples directory and build the library. Every subfolder whose name begins
with code::folderPrefix:: (default code::"breaks"::) is read, so
code::breaks/::, code::breaks175/:: and code::breaks-jungle/:: all qualify. If the
directory you pass has no such subfolders but does contain audio itself, it is
treated as a breaks folder directly.

Each file is loaded as a mono buffer (see code::monoMode::), given a key derived
from its filename, and tagged with an inferred tempo and beat length that a
code::_breaks.scd:: sidecar in the same folder may override.

Calling code::scan:: again frees the previous buffers and starts over.

argument:: dir
the samples directory (or a breaks folder). Tilde paths are expanded.
argument:: server
defaults to code::Server.default::.
returns:: this
discussion::
code::
Break.scan("~/Studio/.../Setup/samples");   // Setup.scd does this for you
::

METHOD:: addLibrary
Index a whole sample library, of any depth, emphasis::without loading it::. Every
folder under code::dir:: that holds audio files becomes a breaks folder: its own
name feeds tempo inference (a folder called code::93 BPM/:: tags every file in it)
and its own code::_breaks.scd:: sidecar applies. Folders whose name is in
code::exclude:: or in code::libraryExclude:: are skipped together with everything
beneath them — that is how folders of one-shot hits stay out of a library of loops.

Nothing reaches the server here. A loop is loaded the first time something plays
it (code::brk::, code::chop::, code::\name.buf:: — all through code::bufOf::), so a
library of a thousand loops costs a thousand file-header reads at boot, well under a
second, instead of gigabytes of memory.

Additive: it adds to whatever is already in the registry. Call it after
code::scan::, which clears the registry first.
argument:: dir
the library's root folder. Tilde paths are expanded.
argument:: exclude
folder names to skip, matched whole and ignoring case (code::"Kicks":: skips a
folder called code::KICKS::, not one called code::Kicks 2::).
argument:: server
defaults to code::Server.default::.
returns:: this
discussion::
code::
Break.scan("~/Studio/.../Setup/samples");
Break.addLibrary("~/Samples/Breaks", exclude: ["Kicks", "Snares"]);
Break.list("funk");    // find something in it
::

METHOD:: bufOf
The mono link::Classes/Buffer:: for a key, loading it now if it was indexed by
code::addLibrary:: and has not played yet. Every word that plays a break goes
through here. The Buffer object comes back at once; its samples reach the server a
moment later — a few tens of milliseconds for a typical loop.

A emphasis::swap:: (code::~d.brk(\other)::) asks for the samples when you evaluate
it and plays them on the next bar, so the load is hidden inside the wait. Only the
very first code::brk:: on a fresh proxy plays straight away, and even that starts on
the next bar under the usual quant; use code::preload:: when a set must open on a
library break with nothing left to chance.
argument:: key
returns:: a Buffer, or nil if the key is unknown or the server is not running

METHOD:: preload
Load library breaks ahead of time — typically the ones a set opens with.
argument::  ... keys
returns:: the Buffers
discussion::
code::
Break.preload(\gold_rush, \searchin_break);
::

METHOD:: list
Post the library as a table: key, bpm, beats, duration, which inference rule fired,
and the filename. The code::from:: column is the important one — it tells you
whether a tempo was read from a tag or guessed from duration.

With a large library, pass a search string: only entries whose key, filename or
folder contains it are posted.
argument:: match
optional. Case is ignored.
returns:: the array of keys posted
discussion::
code::
Break.list;              // everything
Break.list("fill");      // just the fills
Break.list("93 bpm");    // everything in a folder called 93 BPM
::

METHOD:: at
The entry for a key, as an link::Classes/Event:: with keys code::buf:: (the mono
link::Classes/Buffer::), code::bpm::, code::beats::, code::dur::, code::path::,
code::file::, code::folder::, code::chans:: and code::src:: (which rule set the
tempo).
argument:: key
a link::Classes/Symbol::.
returns:: an Event, or nil

METHOD:: set
Override a break's tempo or beat length and persist it to the folder's
code::_breaks.scd:: sidecar. Any live voice playing that break picks the new length
up at once. Usually reached as code::\amen.bpm_(172):: / code::\amen.len_(8)::.

Passing only code::bpm:: recomputes beats from the duration; passing only
code::beats:: recomputes bpm.
argument:: key
argument:: bpm
argument:: beats
returns:: the updated entry

METHOD:: capture
Register an already-recorded buffer as a break. A capture made at the session
tempo needs no inference — you know its beat count exactly — which makes this the
most reliable entry in the library.
argument:: key
argument:: buf
a mono link::Classes/Buffer::.
argument:: beats
its length in beats.
returns:: the key actually used (deduplicated if the name was taken)
discussion::
code::
r = rec(8);                    // Synesthesia's live sampler
Break.capture(\me, r, 8);
~e.brk(\me);
::

METHOD:: inferTempo
The tempo guess, as a pure function — exposed because it is the part most likely to
be wrong about your particular files, and the part worth testing. Returns an
link::Classes/Event:: with code::bpm::, code::beats:: and code::src::.

Priority: a bpm-tagged number in the filename (code::src: \filename::), then a
tagged or plausible number in the folder name (code::\folder::), then the duration
heuristic (code::\duration::, or code::\hint:: when a bare filename number broke a
tie, or code::\fallback:: when nothing was plausible).
argument:: fileName
argument:: folderName
argument:: dur
duration in seconds.
returns:: an Event

METHOD:: bpmTags
Every digit run in a string, each flagged with whether it sits next to "bpm". This
is what stops a filename like code::250093__amfe__175bpm-loop.wav:: from being read
as 250093 bpm.
returns:: an array of Events with code::num:: and code::tagged::

METHOD:: beatsFor
How many beats a loop of code::dur:: seconds holds at code::bpm::, rounded. Warns
when the result is far from a whole number, which means either the tag is wrong or
the file is not cut to whole beats.
argument:: bpm
argument:: dur

METHOD:: bpmFromDur
The duration heuristic on its own: assume a whole number of beats, prefer the
reading whose tempo is most plausible. code::hint:: (a bare number from the
filename) only breaks ties — except for a whole track, longer than 32 beats at any
plausible tempo, where there is no reading to choose between and a plausible
code::hint:: is taken as the tempo outright.
argument:: dur
argument:: hint

METHOD:: parseSeq
Parse a slice sequence. In a link::Classes/String::, digits then letters index
slices (base 36), code::.:: and code::~:: are rests, spaces and code::|:: are
ignored. An link::Classes/Array:: passes through, with nil, negative and
link::Classes/Rest:: entries becoming rests. Rests are code::-1::.
returns:: an array of integers
discussion::
code::
Break.parseSeq("0.325");        // [ 0, -1, 3, 2, 5 ]
Break.parseSeq("9ab");          // [ 9, 10, 11 ]
Break.parseSeq([0, nil, 3]);    // [ 0, -1, 3 ]
::

METHOD:: stretchOf
The playback rate that makes a break fit the current session tempo. Used by
code::vari:: on the engine and by code::chop::.
argument:: key

METHOD:: tempo
The performance clock's tempo in beats per second. emphasis::Setting:: it —
code::Break.tempo_(bps):: — pushes that tempo to every live break voice;
code::bpm():: calls this for immediacy, and a link::Classes/SkipJack:: watcher calls
it for tempo changes made any other way.
argument:: bps
when setting: beats per second.
returns:: when getting: beats per second

METHOD:: window
The bufnum of a named grain envelope: code::\punch:: (fast attack, short tail —
keeps drum transients), code::\gate:: (near-rectangular), code::\hann:: (smooth,
classic granular), code::\swell:: (reversed). Reached as code::~d.win(\gate)::.
argument:: name

METHOD:: register
Install the code::\break:: engine on a proxy, allocate its slice-sequence buffer
and wire up its infrastructure controls. code::brk:: calls this; you should not
need to.
argument:: proxy
argument:: key
returns:: the voice Event (code::key::, code::seq::, code::steps::, code::slen::, code::ordered::)

METHOD:: unregister
Forget a proxy and free its sequence buffer.
argument:: proxy

METHOD:: writeSeq
Write a slice sequence into a voice's buffer, set its active length, and realign to
the bar. code::ordered:: marks the sequence as plain source order so that a later
change of code::steps:: can safely regenerate it.
argument:: proxy
argument:: values
argument:: ordered

METHOD:: trig
Fire a trigger-rate control on a proxy exactly once.

warning::
This sets the control and then immediately unsets it, and that second step is
load-bearing. link::Classes/NodeProxy::'s code::set:: stores values in the proxy's
nodeMap, which is replayed on every rebuild — so a trigger control left set to 1
fires again every time the proxy is re-evaluated or faded in.
::
argument:: proxy
argument:: key

METHOD:: schedBar
Run a function on the next quant boundary of the performance clock, so structural
changes land musically. Falls back to running immediately if there is no clock or
quant is 0.
argument:: func

METHOD:: freeAll
Free every buffer (library, sequences, windows), forget every voice and stop the
tempo watcher.

METHOD:: clear
Free the library's buffers and empty it, leaving voices and windows alone.

METHOD:: resolveKey
Resolve a break reference: nil gives the one after code::current:: (which is how
code::~d.brk:: cycles), an link::Classes/Integer:: indexes code::order::, anything
else is treated as a name.
argument:: name
argument:: current

SUBSECTION:: Settings

METHOD:: monoMode
How a multichannel file becomes the mono buffer code::GrainBuf:: requires.
code::\mix:: (default) is a true mixdown, done in the language with array slicing;
code::\left:: and code::\right:: take one channel via
link::Classes/Buffer#*readChannel::.

METHOD:: folderPrefix
Which subfolders code::scan:: treats as breaks. Default code::"breaks"::.

METHOD:: seqSize
Frames in a slice-sequence buffer, i.e. the longest sequence and the most slices.
Default 64.

METHOD:: bpmWindow
The plausible source-tempo range the duration heuristic works within. Default
code::[60, 200]::.

METHOD:: audioExtensions
Readable file extensions. Default code::["wav", "aiff", "aif", "flac"]:: — mp3 is
not readable by link::Classes/Buffer::.

METHOD:: libraryExclude
Folder names code::addLibrary:: always skips, on top of its own code::exclude::
list. Default code::["chops", "one shots", "one-shots", "oneshots"]:: — the names
sample packs give their pre-sliced hits. A slice is a one-shot, not a loop, and the
engine makes its own slices anyway.

SUBSECTION:: State

METHOD:: all
The library: key -> entry.

METHOD:: order
Keys in scan order. code::~d.brk:: with no argument cycles through this.

METHOD:: voices
Live break voices: proxy -> voice Event.

METHOD:: windows
Grain-envelope buffers by name.

METHOD:: size
How many breaks are loaded.

METHOD:: keyAt
The key at an index, wrapping.

METHOD:: server
The server the library loads onto.

EXAMPLES::

code::
// the whole thing, from nothing
s.waitForBoot({
    var b = Buffer.read(s, Platform.resourceDir +/+ "sounds/a11wlk01.wav",
        action: { |buf|
            Break.capture(\demo, buf, 2);   // 2 beats at 120 bpm
            Break.list;
        });
});

// play it
p = ProxySpace.push(s); p.makeTempoClock; p.clock.tempo = 2; p.quant = 4;
~d.brk(\demo); ~d.play;

// sculpt it
~d.grind(0.75);      // toward grains
~d.seq("0.1.");      // a sparser order
~d.scr(0.4);         // let chance in
~d.ord;              // back to source order

// inspect what the registry decided
Break.at(\demo);
Break.list;
::
